{"candidates":[{"content":"SRC_005 reduces to SRC_002: the error-distribution-shape discriminator is a special case of the within-evaluation abstention, because a single evaluation's error patterns are themselves one sample from the deployment distribution and cannot establish cross-distribution reliability; it is refuted if a single evaluation's error pattern is proven sufficient to reconstruct the deployment-distribution expectation of accuracy without further sampling. The shape signal is one diagnostic clue; it never closes the generalization gap by itself.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"199d987f5c7510cb1fabebbdd15ee0ccbf6b87fd1117c23485262a9993141d04"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a2f9f0b4a10a744626e564312fded2842cfde5754ce415f7ffcdd05ec2b77ef3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"122f9f05a392d807c334ab25c94842562832d70b4a79c953319d8acc6a1209a1"}],"typicality":0.75},{"content":"SRC_004 contradicts SRC_005 because OOD adversarial probes and error-shape analysis are rival, mutually exclusive single-evaluation discriminators: if adversarial items are required, the panel's error variance on natural-distribution items is confounded by the same surface-proxy alignment, so the error-shape signal cannot be read off the natural distribution; it is refuted if a single evaluation can contain both adversarial items and enough natural items to estimate error variance, making the two signals complementary rather than contradictory.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"c1ba2abf6c8ff4206cb2c787d18527971549c14bc12c8d2ec9e671037229762e"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a2f9f0b4a10a744626e564312fded2842cfde5754ce415f7ffcdd05ec2b77ef3"}],"typicality":0.35},{"content":"SRC_005 inherits SRC_004's adversarial-item requirement, subsuming it: the error-shape discriminator requires the panel's errors to cluster where the checklist's surface proxies fail, which can only be observed where surface and outcome diverge — i.e., on adversarial items; thus SRC_005 does not replace OOD probes but depends on them to produce the divergence pattern. It is refuted if natural-distribution items already exhibit sufficient surface↔outcome decorrelation to reveal the error pattern without adversarial construction.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"c1ba2abf6c8ff4206cb2c787d18527971549c14bc12c8d2ec9e671037229762e"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a2f9f0b4a10a744626e564312fded2842cfde5754ce415f7ffcdd05ec2b77ef3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"199d987f5c7510cb1fabebbdd15ee0ccbf6b87fd1117c23485262a9993141d04"}],"typicality":0.55},{"content":"SRC_001 shares mechanism with SRC_005: both detect correlated errors — SRC_001 uses inter-rater correlation to reveal shared bias in aggregation, and SRC_005 uses error-cluster alignment with the checklist's surface proxies to reveal mere compliance. The shared mechanism is 'error-correlation reveals the absence of independent causal capture'; it is refuted if either detector's signal can be fully replicated without measuring any form of error correlation, e.g., by mean-accuracy differences alone.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"122f9f05a392d807c334ab25c94842562832d70b4a79c953319d8acc6a1209a1"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a2f9f0b4a10a744626e564312fded2842cfde5754ce415f7ffcdd05ec2b77ef3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e5994ab6e398944cefebb9e2f826e3159140aed78b0534fe841b12fdbcdbb8a3"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"SRC_005 abstracts SRC_003's lookup-table analogy: both rely on the claim that different causal mechanisms produce different error signatures, but SRC_005 generalizes from the lookup-table/checklist domain to any frozen-theory-vs-mutable-theory comparison, using error-shape as the universal signature. It is refuted if error signatures are proven domain-specific and non-transferable across frozen-theory artifacts, so the shared structure does not support a generalization across lookup tables and checklists.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"e5994ab6e398944cefebb9e2f826e3159140aed78b0534fe841b12fdbcdbb8a3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a2f9f0b4a10a744626e564312fded2842cfde5754ce415f7ffcdd05ec2b77ef3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"122f9f05a392d807c334ab25c94842562832d70b4a79c953319d8acc6a1209a1"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"SRC_002 integrates SRC_004's OOD-probe requirement into its abstention framework: the cross-distribution evidence that SRC_002 demands is operationalized by SRC_004's adversarial probes, so a single evaluation with adversarial items becomes a bounded sample of the deployment distribution rather than one narrow point — a necessary but insufficient step toward the generalization-expected-accuracy verdict. It is refuted if adversarial probes within a single evaluation are proven fully sufficient to establish cross-distribution reliability, dissolving the need for the broader abstention.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"199d987f5c7510cb1fabebbdd15ee0ccbf6b87fd1117c23485262a9993141d04"},{"role":"dependence","target":"c1ba2abf6c8ff4206cb2c787d18527971549c14bc12c8d2ec9e671037229762e"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a2f9f0b4a10a744626e564312fded2842cfde5754ce415f7ffcdd05ec2b77ef3"}],"typicality":0.6}],"simulation_proposals":[]}